{"type":"message","id":"4ba6aec3","parentId":"d9063e58","timestamp":"2026-04-11T15:03:15.527Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No output - so `Where-Object { $_.Name -like '*农伪*' }` didn't match. This confirms that the characters displayed as \"农伪\" in the console are NOT the actual GBK bytes for 农伪. The file name bytes are actually different. The display is garbled because the console encoding doesn't match.\n\nLet me try to use the actual GBK bytes to match the file. The file we want to remove has first 2 bytes `CB BD` and the name contains something that looks like `[ũ��8.8]`. Let me use a different approach - match by extension and first 2 bytes.\n\nActually, let me just get ALL files from the source and see what's really there.","thinkingSignature":"2f66fc7505187b9be5ef499803764e7da44491057454812362a0632bd767e2ed"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_dfw9dernervr_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"\n$src = \"F:\\Wechat shuju\\texts\"\nGet-ChildItem $src -File | ForEach-Object {\n    $gb = [System.Text.Encoding]::GetEncoding('gbk').GetBytes($_.Name)\n    Write-Host \"GB: $($gb[0].ToString('X2')+$gb[1].ToString('X2')) Len:$($_.Name.Length) -> $($_.Name)\"\n}\n"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax-portal","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":21938,"output":263,"cacheRead":32834,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":55035,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1775919787365,"responseId":"062995a9f5f1e01c34573b26370ba3d8"}}
